# Git does not read this by default. Point it here once:
#
#     git config gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile .allowed_signers
#
# after which `git log --show-signature` and `git verify-commit <rev>`
# will report signatures from these keys as good.
#
# Format: <principal> <namespaces> <key>. The principal is matched against
# the committer's email, and the `git` namespace is what commit
# signatures use.
#
# Entries are separated with `#` rather than blank lines. `ssh-keygen -Y
# find-principals`, which `git verify-commit` calls, reports an empty
# line as `missing key`. It still verifies, but it warns every time.
